  {
   "id": "k2-58",
   "name": "Conductors and non-conductors (insulators)"
  }
 ],
 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Summary of how substances behave with electricity:\n• Conductors that are not changed: metals and graphite (solid). Electrons carry the current.\n• Conductors that are decomposed (electrolytes): molten ionic compounds and solutions of acids, bases and salts. Ions carry the current.\n• Non-conductors (insulators): non-metals (except graphite), plastics, rubber, glass, dry wood, porcelain; covalent liquids such as ethanol, kerosene, pure (distilled) water and sugar solution.\nPure water is a very poor conductor, but tap water and rain water conduct a little because they contain dissolved salts. That is why it is dangerous to touch switches with wet hands.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {},
  "problem": "Why is it dangerous to use an electric appliance with wet hands, although pure water is a poor conductor?",
  "steps": [
   "Tap water and sweat contain dissolved salts.",
   "These ions let the water conduct, so current can pass through you."
  ],
  "answer": "Because ordinary water contains dissolved salts and conducts.",
  "checks": []
 },
